    {
      "id": 36,
      "type": "logs",
      "title": "Time Machine logs",
      "description": "Two disjoint sources. The first is smbd's connect/disconnect audit for the timemachine share (share|user|ip|op), which is the only record of a Mac actually showing up: a backup that never ran leaves nothing anywhere else, since the panels above read the sparsebundle on @TM_PATH@ and a bundle that is not written simply keeps its old mtime. A connect immediately followed by a disconnect means the Mac mounted the share and gave up without writing. The second is smbd's own sparsebundle-level complaints (fruit_* and anything naming a sparsebundle) — those lines carry no share label, because the journal pipeline only extracts one from the pipe-prefixed audit format, so they need a selector of their own. What is deliberately *not* here is smbd's unpack_canon_ace chatter: macOS sets an ACL per file and smbd logs one pair of level-0 lines for each, which would bury everything above — read samba-smbd.service directly for that.",
      "datasource": { "type": "loki", "uid": "loki" },
      "gridPos": { "x": 0, "y": 65, "w": 24, "h": 9 },
      "options": {
        "showTime": true,
        "sortOrder": "Descending",
        "wrapLogMessage": true
      },
      "targets": [
        {
          "datasource": { "type": "loki", "uid": "loki" },
          "expr": "{share=\"timemachine\"}",
          "refId": "A"
        },
        {
          "datasource": { "type": "loki", "uid": "loki" },
          "expr": "{unit=\"samba-smbd.service\"} |~ \"sparsebundle|fruit_\"",
          "refId": "B"
        }
      ]
    }
